export const DE_C1_S07_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-die-letzte-runde": [
    `Uncle Sepp had planned his funeral the way he had planned the church fair at the Gasthof Wieser for forty-three years: down to the last detail, with a schedule, a seating plan and a list of who had to bring what. Theresa found the envelope two days after his death in the drawer under the bar, between beer mats and a roll of toilet paper. On it, in his scrawly handwriting, was written: "For Resi. Read out at the funeral. Loud."`,
    `Theresa had not been called Resi for fifteen years. At nineteen she had gone to Vienna, studied business administration, and now worked for a management consultancy, where she had been taught to declare inns like the Wieser unprofitable. She came to the Gail Valley at Christmas and for funerals, and every time she was a little relieved when the train pulled out of Villach again. Her uncle had left her the inn. She had already called the estate agent in Klagenfurt before she found the envelope.`,
    `On the day of the funeral it rained, as is only proper. Half the valley stood in the cemetery: the mayor, the fire brigade in uniform, the Thursday card group, Frau Pirker from the bakery, with whom her uncle had supposedly never had anything going on. After the blessing Theresa stepped up to the grave, unfolded the sheet and read, in the voice she used for presentations in Vienna: "Dear mourners. If you're hearing this, I'm dead and you're wet. The one I can't change, the other you could have, because I told you a hundred times: in the Gail Valley it rains at every funeral."`,
    `A few people laughed. The priest looked at the ground. Theresa read on, and soon understood that her uncle had no intention of comforting anyone. He was settling scores. The mayor, it said, had owed the Gasthof Wieser eleven glasses of wine and a goulash since the election party in 2009, and would he please sort that out with Resi. The fire chief should finally admit that the fire in the hay barn in 1998 had come from his own cigarette; everyone had always known anyway. And he thanked Frau Pirker for thirty years of croissants, and only for the croissants, so that this was said for once too.`,
    `Now the whole cemetery laughed, even the mayor, if a little sourly. Frau Pirker cried and laughed at the same time. Theresa noticed that her presentation voice had disappeared; she was reading now the way people talk in the valley, slowly and with all the vowels, and she had no idea when that had happened. Then came the last paragraph, and the cemetery went quiet.`,
    `"And now to Resi," she read. "Resi will want to sell the inn. She's right, it doesn't pay, it never has. But an inn doesn't have to pay, Resi; an inn is the place where people go when they can't go anywhere else. Go ahead and sell it. But first you'll stand behind the bar for one summer. Just one. If you still want to after that, call the estate agent, Dr Rainer in Klagenfurt, he's asked me three times already anyway. Give him my best. He's a crook."`,
    `Afterwards, as the schedule said, everyone went to the inn. According to the will, the first round was on the deceased, the second on the mayor, "he knows why". Theresa had not intended to stand behind the bar. But the waitress was over eighty and crying, the fire brigade was thirsty, and somebody had to pull the beer. She pulled it badly, with far too much foam, and the fire chief said that was just how Sepp had done it at the beginning, in 1981, and he meant it as a compliment.`,
    `Towards midnight, when the last of them had gone, Theresa sat alone in the dining room among empty glasses, with damp hair and a tea towel over her shoulder. On her phone there was a message from the estate agent: was she free for a viewing tomorrow? She read it twice. Then she wrote back that tomorrow unfortunately wasn't possible, and the summer wasn't either, she had to work. Only when she put the phone down did she notice that she had signed it "Resi".`,
  ],
  "de-die-vierte-am-tisch": [
    `For forty years they had played Jass every Thursday evening at the Restaurant Schwanen in Thun, the four of them, at the round table next to the tiled stove: Fritz Ammann, a retired train driver; Walter Bühler, who used to run the chemist's on the Rathausplatz; Hansruedi Zbinden, a dentist; and Ernst Kunz, a carpenter, who won most of the games and never revealed how. In January Ernst died, at eighty-six, while shovelling snow in front of his workshop, which everyone considered a fitting death for him, except his wife.`,
    `On the first Thursday after the funeral the three who were left sat at the round table and looked at the empty chair. You can't play a Schieber with three; there are games for three, but that is, as Walter said, like playing a string quartet with three violins. They decided to look for a fourth. It was harder than they thought. The candidate from the male choir counted too slowly, the one from the shooting club talked too much, and Hansruedi's grandson, a computer scientist, worked out the probabilities in his head and won, which disqualified him immediately.`,
    `In March Margrit Kunz stood in the doorway of the Schwanen. She was wearing the grey coat she had worn at the funeral and holding a pack of cards that the men recognised at once: Ernst's cards, with the Swiss German suits, bells, roses, shields and acorns, as soft as cloth at the corners. "You're looking for a fourth," she said. "I hear it's not going well."`,
    `The men looked at one another. For forty years Margrit had stayed at home every Thursday evening. She had never set foot in the Schwanen, at least not on a Thursday. Fritz cleared his throat and said, as kindly as he could, that it was very nice of her, but the Schieber was a demanding game, you had to count the cards, be able to read your partner, and Ernst had surely never... He broke off because Margrit had already sat down. On Ernst's chair.`,
    `She played with Hansruedi against the other two. After the first round they were a hundred and forty points ahead. After the third Walter had stopped talking, which had not happened in forty years. Margrit played fast and without hesitating, and she played exactly the way Ernst had played: she held back her trumps until it hurt, and she always knew what was still in play. At about eleven Fritz put his cards down. "Margrit," he said, "where did you learn that?"`,
    `She took a sip of her white wine and smiled. In the early years, she said, Ernst had played miserably. Every Thursday he had come home with empty pockets and in a bad mood. So she, who as a child had played every evening with her father up on the Alp, had begun to practise with him, at the kitchen table, every Wednesday. For forty years, on Wednesdays, they had replayed the games of the Thursday before, card by card, because Ernst had a memory like an elephant, only unfortunately no instinct.`,
    `"So for forty years he beat us with your tricks," said Hansruedi slowly. "With my tricks," said Margrit, "and with his hands. He had beautiful hands for shuffling." Nobody said anything. Walter, who in forty years had not won a single time when Ernst was his opponent, stared at his cards as if they had betrayed him personally. Then he began to laugh, so loudly that the waitress turned round, and the others laughed with him, Margrit the longest.`,
    `Since then they have once again played every Thursday, the four of them, at the round table next to the tiled stove. Margrit wins most of the games and never reveals how. Only on Wednesday evenings, so people in Thun say, a light still burns in the Kunz family's kitchen, and anyone who stops by the window can hear cards slapping down on the table, one after another, as if someone were replaying a game with a partner you can't see.`,
  ],
  "de-antrag-auf-musse": [
    `The Act for the Promotion of Leisure came into force on 1 January 2041, after a commission set up by the Bundestag had found that the average German had not spent a single minute without a recognisable purpose for fifteen years. Even a walk, according to the report, now served one's step count, reading served self-optimisation and doing nothing served recovery, in other words, ultimately, work. A people that could no longer be bored, it said on page 412, would sooner or later no longer think either.`,
    `Since then every citizen has been entitled to four hours of state-recognised leisure a week. The entitlement must, however, be applied for, namely at the newly founded Federal Office for Leisure and Deceleration in Bonn, of which people in Berlin said mockingly that it was the first authority in the republic to have fulfilled its mission before it was even founded.`,
    `Olaf Wendt, 51, a logistics planner from Bad Godesberg, submitted his application in February. Form M-7 ran to nine pages. Under item 4 ("Type and extent of the intended inactivity") he wrote: "Sit on a bench by the Rhine and look at the water." Three weeks later he received a notice asking him to be more specific: whether he intended to listen to music or podcasts while looking at the water (not permitted, as it counts as taking in information), whether he intended to count the ships (not permitted, as data collection) and whether the sitting served to relieve his back (not permitted, as health care).`,
    `Wendt was more specific. He would hear nothing but the water, count nothing and keep his back hunched. The second notice came in April. He was informed that the Rhine near Bonn was a federal waterway and that the consent of the responsible shipping authority would therefore have to be obtained; furthermore, it had to be clarified whether the bench on which he intended to sit was municipal property. One Sunday afternoon Wendt drove to the riverbank to photograph the bench and realised that, strictly speaking, he was doing something again.`,
    `In June he was invited to a personal interview. He waited on the ground floor of the federal office, in a corridor with pale green walls and a window onto the inner courtyard, where a lime tree stood. The queue number on the screen changed only after forty minutes, then not at all. Wendt had left his phone at home. He had nothing to read. First he looked at the clock, then at the lime tree, then only at the lime tree. A blackbird landed in it and flew off again. Later a second one came, or the same one. The light slowly moved across the wall. At some point he noticed that he hadn't looked at the clock for ages, and he deliberately didn't look so as not to spoil it.`,
    `At about half past four a clerk called him in, Frau Ostermann, a woman with reading glasses and tired, friendly eyes. She leafed through his file, sighed and asked how long he had waited. Wendt said he didn't know exactly. Frau Ostermann looked at him over her glasses for a long time, and then typed something into her computer.`,
    `The decision came a week later, by post, on recycled paper. His application for leisure on the riverbank was rejected, as coordination with the authorities involved would probably continue until 2043. However, the review had shown that on 14 June, in the office's waiting area, he had spent four hours and twelve minutes in a state that fully met the criteria of Section 2. This time would be credited to him retroactively. They wished him a restful remainder of the year.`,
    `Wendt has not renewed his application. Since the summer, though, he goes to the federal office every other Thursday, takes a queue number and sits down in the pale green corridor. He is not the only one. In September there were seven of them; in November chairs had to be fetched from the first floor. Frau Ostermann now calls the numbers very slowly. Sometimes, people say, she doesn't call them at all.`,
  ],
  "de-niedrigwasser": [
    `Jens Ohlsen had been leading groups across the mudflats for thirty-one years, from Duhnen over to Neuwerk, twelve kilometres of silt, tidal channels and sky, and in that time he had developed a reliable typology of humanity. There were those who walked too fast and those who took too many photos. There were those who wanted to go barefoot and changed their minds after two kilometres of mussel shells. And for some years now there had been those with apps.`,
    `Mattis was eleven, came from Hanover and had one. At the meeting point he was already holding his phone under Jens's nose: low tide at 11.52, the app said; Jens had 12.10 on his sheet, and that was wrong. Jens looked at the screen, then at the boy, then at the horizon. The app was calculating with the tide gauge on Heligoland, he said, and Heligoland unfortunately wasn't on the way. Mattis's mother laughed apologetically. Mattis didn't laugh. He had, you could see, decided to keep an eye on Jens.`,
    `The first few kilometres went the way they always went. Jens pointed out lugworms and their coils, cockles, a shore crab that raised its pincers and refused to serve as a teaching aid. Mattis commented. The shore crab was actually called Carcinus maenas. According to the satellite image, the channel ahead of them was forty metres wide, not thirty. Jens let him talk. Thirty-one years, he thought, and now this: a child who knew the mudflats better than he did without ever having set foot on them. Perhaps it was time. In the autumn he would stop; he had promised his wife, and for the first time that promise didn't feel like a defeat.`,
    `The fog came at kilometre seven, the way it comes on the North Sea: not as weather but as a decision. A moment ago you could still see the lighthouse on Neuwerk, grey and comforting on the horizon, and then there was nothing but a milky white in which your own hand looked strange. The group huddled together. Somebody asked how much further it was. Somebody else pulled out a phone, and then everyone pulled out their phones, as if you could swipe the fog away.`,
    `Mattis's app showed a blue dot on a yellow area. The dot moved when Mattis moved, and it also moved when Mattis stood still, ten metres to the left one moment, twenty to the right the next. "The GPS is going haywire," said the boy, and for the first time that day his voice sounded the way an eleven-year-old's voice ought to sound. Jens nodded. Out here, he said, everything went haywire sooner or later. You just had to know what didn't.`,
    `What didn't were the pricken: thin birch saplings stuck in the mud at intervals of fifty metres to mark the way, for as long as the way had existed. In this fog you could only ever see one. Jens lined the group up, each person with a hand on the shoulder of the one in front, and went ahead until the next marker appeared out of the white. Then he called, the line followed, and he went on. It was slow, it was laborious, and it was, he knew, completely safe as long as nobody lost their nerve.`,
    `At the third marker Mattis stopped beside him. "Wait," he said. "Listen." Jens heard nothing but the squelching of rubber boots. Then he did hear it: bells, very faint, from somewhere to the left. The mudflat carts. The horse-drawn wagons that ran between Duhnen and Neuwerk at every low tide, and whose horses, as everyone knew, wore little bells on their harnesses. If you could hear the bells, you were almost there. Jens would have heard them too, of course, in a few minutes. But the boy had heard them first.`,
    `They reached Neuwerk half an hour later than planned, wet to the knees, with red faces and the silly pride of people who have survived an adventure that wasn't one. In the island garden, over tea, Mattis sat down next to Jens. He had done the sums again, he said. Low tide really had been at 12.10. Jens nodded and drank his tea. Then from the inside pocket of his jacket he pulled a dog-eared notebook of handwritten tide tables, thirty-one years of pencil, and pushed it across the table to the boy. "In case," he said, "your battery ever runs out."`,
  ],
  "de-die-laufmaschine": [
    `Mannheim, 13 June 1817. Dearest sister, in your last letter you ask whether it is true, what people are saying in Heidelberg, namely that our lodger on the second floor, the Baron von Drais, rode out to Schwetzingen yesterday on a wooden horse with no head and no legs. I will answer you as well as I can and ask you not to pass it on, for people in town already consider ours a house where things are not quite right.`,
    `You know what last year was like. It rained from May until October, in July snow fell in the Odenwald, the grain rotted in the fields, and oats now cost what a calf used to cost. People have slaughtered their horses because they could no longer feed them, and whoever has no horse goes on foot. The baron says that is precisely why he built his machine: a vehicle that eats no oats. I told him it ate none because it didn't go. I must confess that I said so with some satisfaction.`,
    `Since the winter he has been sawing, planing and cursing in the courtyard. The thing that came out of it consists of two wooden wheels, one behind the other, and a beam between them that you sit on like a saddle. At the front there is a bar with which you can turn the front wheel. You push yourself off the ground with your feet, alternately, as in walking, except that you are sitting. I asked him why a sensible person should walk sitting down when he can already do it standing up. He replied that I understood nothing about balance. That may be. But never in my life have I seen anything stay upright on two wheels without falling over, and until recently neither had he.`,
    `All through May he practised in the courtyard, and the children from the neighbourhood sat on the wall and hooted. He fell over more than twenty times; I counted. Weidner the baker from across the street bet me a guilder that before the summer was over the baron would be lying in the Rhine together with his machine. I took the bet, not out of trust in the machine, but because I can't stand Weidner.`,
    `So yesterday morning he set off, out along the country road to the south as far as the relay station where the mail coaches change horses, and back again. A messenger followed him on horseback to bear witness. For someone on foot that is a good three hours' walk there and back. The baron was back in the courtyard in less than an hour, with dust up to his knees and a face like a schoolboy's. The messenger, dearest, was out of breath. So was the horse.`,
    `I got my guilder from Weidner, and he looked as if he had bitten into a sour apple. In the evening the baron offered to let me try it myself. I declined, pointing to my skirts and my age. In truth, and I am telling only you, this morning, before anyone was up, I fetched the machine out of the shed and sat on it. I got as far as the fountain. I have a bruised knee and a torn hem, but I got as far as the fountain.`,
    `The baron now wants to have it put in the newspaper that his running machine will one day replace horses, and to travel with it through the whole Grand Duchy. I don't believe anything will come of it. Who would voluntarily sit on a thing that falls over as soon as you stop moving? That isn't a vehicle, that's a piece of wisdom about life, and people have never wanted to know much about those. In ten years, sister, nobody will be talking about it.`,
    `Give my regards to your husband and the children. I have put the guilder in my money box, for if something does come of it after all, I want to be the first woman in Mannheim to buy one of those things. Your loving sister Margarethe.`,
  ],
};

